Introduction

Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH) is a clinically elusive rare orthopedic disorder with a global prevalence of approximately 1%–3%. While DDH primarily affects children, recent studies have uncovered a significant proportion of adults with undiagnosed childhood onset, leading to severe hip degeneration and substantial functional impairment. This issue is particularly acute in resource-limited regions, where delayed diagnosis and intervention prevail.

Traditional radiographic keypoint assessment for hip X-rays relies heavily on radiologist expertise and manual operations, resulting in time-consuming and often inconsistent evaluations. Despite significant progress in AI-driven medical image analysis, most existing systems remain opaque in reasoning and offer limited utility to non-orthopedic clinicians and patients in interpreting complex anatomical metrics (e.g., center-edge and Sharp angles). Furthermore, care coordination and rehabilitation management for DDH patients remain fragmented, with a lack of integrated telemedicine platforms providing end-to-end service continuity—ultimately hampering both adherence and outcomes.

To address these unmet clinical and technical challenges, we present HipGo—a remote DDH diagnosis and management platform for adolescents and adults, powered by a multi-module intelligent medical agent framework. HipGo integrates a hybrid convolutional neural network (CNN) and graph attention network (GAT) for precise hip keypoint localization, multimodal clinical report generation combining structured diagnostic metrics and deep visual features, and modules for intelligent decision support and remote rehabilitation management. The system aims to close the loop from accurate, explainable diagnosis to personalized recovery in a fully automated and accessible manner.

Major contributions of this study include:
- Harnessing 800 heterogeneous hip X-rays crowdsourced via Chinese social media, with an active learning pipeline to boost annotation efficiency and model robustness;
- Introducing a CNN-GAT feature fusion model with anatomical priors, substantially improving keypoint detection precision and clinical angle measurement fidelity;
- Designing a multimodal automatic report generator that enhances interpretability and practical value for clinical diagnostics;
- Implementing an end-to-end intelligent agent platform for seamless online-to-offline integration and equitable access to expert care.

Related Work

Recent years have witnessed significant advances in medical AI spanning image analysis, automatic report generation, and intelligent diagnostic platforms. Here, we review the technological landscape along four principal axes relevant to remote DDH management, thereby establishing the theoretical foundations underpinning HipGo’s innovations.

2.1 Keypoint Detection and Spatial Modeling in Medical Imaging

Anatomical keypoint detection forms the bedrock of AI-driven orthopedic diagnosis. Early methods relied on conventional visual descriptors (e.g., SIFT, HOG), but these approaches struggled with complex medical anatomies. With the advent of deep learning, convolutional neural networks (CNNs) have become the dominant paradigm, particularly multiscale feature extractors based on ResNet and HRNet. HRNet, in particular, achieves high precision for anatomical landmark localization by maintaining high-resolution representations. Nonetheless, pure CNNs are limited in modeling spatial relationships between keypoints—an essential factor for capturing complex pelvic topology.

To remedy these limitations, graph neural networks (GNNs) have been introduced into medical image analysis. Recent self-supervised keypoint detection frameworks using depth distillation enhance feature representation, while graph attention network (GAT)-based approaches dynamically learn inter-point dependencies. Hybrid CNN-GNN architectures have demonstrated superior spatial consistency in tasks like brain MRI registration and retinal image analysis. However, most methods are tuned for standardized images, falling short in adapting to heterogenous, low-quality images from non-standard sources like social media, and lack tailored anatomical constraints for rare disorders such as DDH.

2.2 Automatic Medical Report Generation and Multimodal Fusion

Medical report generation has evolved from rule-based templates and expert knowledge bases—rigid but accurate in terminology—to encoder–decoder deep learning models, mapping visual input to text. Multimodal fusion, integrating image features, structured data, and clinical context, has measurably advanced the completeness and accuracy of generated reports. Frameworks such as REMOTE leverage self-training to match full-supervision performance using just 1% labeled data. The advent of large language models (LLMs) has further enhanced report fluency and interpretability. However, current systems are largely limited to common scenarios (e.g., chest X-ray), and their ability to interpret specialized geometric descriptors—critical for DDH—is inadequate for high-precision diagnostics.

2.3 Intelligent Medical Agents and Multi-Agent Systems

Intelligent agent systems mark a paradigm shift in medical informatics, with multi-agent systems (MAS) modeling collaborative workflows among physicians, patients, and devices for distributed, autonomous, and interoperable healthcare management. MAS-driven AI systems improve decision support, personalized treatment, and real-time monitoring, enhancing accuracy and efficiency in care delivery. Nevertheless, adoption at scale is constrained by persistent issues of data bias, interoperability, and responsibility allocation. Dedicated agent architectures for complex disease management remain underdeveloped, with most existing systems targeting generic applications and lacking the specificity and full-loop integration required for rare diseases.

2.4 Remote Management Platforms for Rare Diseases

Rare disease tele-management faces unique hurdles: dispersed patient populations and limited specialist access. The EU’s Clinical Patient Management System (CPMS 2.0) now supports remote consultations across 24 reference networks for over 30 million patients, facilitating expert resource sharing and improved access. Remote patient monitoring (RPM)—via wearables, mobile health apps, and cloud data infrastructure—enables continuous health data collection and real-time analytics, especially for long-term care, allowing early warning and personalized intervention. Digital therapeutics platforms like Mahalo Health and OpenApp’s Clinical Insight registry provide scalable, precise management for rare conditions. However, existing platforms are often narrowly focused, lacking integrated diagnostic intelligence or robust end-to-end clinical pathways.

2.5 Technological Trends and Innovation Gaps

Medical AI is trending towards multimodal fusion, intelligent collaboration, and hyper-personalized services. Yet, the remote care of orthopedic rare diseases such as DDH remains underserved, with notable gaps:
(1) absence of robust keypoint detection techniques for non-standard images,
(2) limited clinical interpretability of automatic reporting for complex geometric metrics,
(3) lack of end-to-end platforms linking imaging, diagnosis, decision-making, and rehabilitation.
By fusing CNN-GAT encoders with anatomical constraints, multimodal report generation, and closed-loop agent architecture, HipGo seeks to fill these voids and provide a systemic solution for tele-intelligent DDH management.

Methods

This section details the technical framework of HipGo’s intelligent remote diagnosis and management platform, built on a “perception–analysis–decision–execution–feedback” feedback loop. The design integrates multi-source data acquisition, CNN-GAT-based keypoint detection, multimodal clinical report generation, and intelligent decision support with rehabilitation management, all aimed at enhancing automation and accessibility in DDH care.

4.1 Data Acquisition and Preprocessing

To capture real-world heterogeneity in remote Chinese primary care, we collected 800 adult anteroposterior pelvic X-rays from domestic social media platforms, spanning a wide range of devices and challenging imaging conditions[Shi et al., 2017]. Nine key anatomical points were meticulously annotated by orthopedic experts in line with DDH diagnostic criteria, ensuring the clinical validity of reference angles (CE, Sharp, Tönnis)[Wiberg, 1939; Sharp, 1961]. Active learning accelerated annotation by prioritizing high-uncertainty examples for expert review[Settles, 2009]. Preprocessing included optical character recognition (OCR)-based de-identification, standardized resizing (512×512 pixels), ImageNet-statistics normalization, and multi-dimensional augmentation (random rotation, translation, Gaussian noise, motion blur) for generalization[Shorten & Khoshgoftaar, 2019].

4.2 Hybrid CNN-GAT Keypoint Detection

Addressing the intricacy of pelvic anatomy in DDH, we propose a dual-branch model blending a ResNet-50 backbone with feature pyramid network (FPN) for multiscale CNN-based feature extraction[He et al., 2016; Lin et al., 2017], and a 9-node GAT graph with adaptive edge construction, encoding normalized distances, sine/cosine of relative angles, and semantic edge labels for adaptive spatial dependency modeling[Veličković et al., 2018; Hamilton et al., 2017]. A lightweight dynamic gating module adaptively weighs CNN and GAT features by image quality, offsetting local instability in low-quality input. Multi-task loss couples keypoint regression to angle-based anatomical constraint terms, enforcing clinical validity and preventing anatomically implausible outputs[Raghu et al., 2019].

4.3 Multimodal Clinical Report Generation

The system fuses structured angle metrics, deep visual features, and patient clinical information via a cross-attention mechanism for semantic alignment[Li et al., 2020; Wang et al., 2022], empowering comprehensive scene understanding. Report generation leverages a pretrained biomedical LLM variant (LLaVA-Med) to produce DDH guideline-compliant, expert-level reports summarizing angle measurements, abnormal findings, diagnostic assessments, and personalized treatment advice. Superimposed keypoint and measurement line visualizations enhance interpretability and interaction[Li et al., 2023].

4.4 Intelligent Decision Support and Rehabilitation Management

Integrating clinical protocols and patient-specific diagnostics, the decision support module formulates individualized care pathways, underpinning shared physician–patient decision-making. Dynamic rehabilitation plans are tailored according to real-time feedback, delivered via mobile prompts and remote monitoring to bolster compliance and outcomes[Chen et al., 2022; Kumar et al., 2022]. All workflow data flows seamlessly through standardized interfaces aligned with electronic health records (EHR), enabling multi-end access and export. Continuous feedback collection and reinforcement learning drive ongoing algorithmic and service optimization.

Experiments & Results

This section validates HipGo’s performance through multidimensional experiments, covering keypoint detection accuracy, clinical angle quantification, and practical system utility in remote DDH care.

5.1 Experimental Design and Datasets

The 800-case X-ray dataset was split by subject into training (640), validation (80), and test (80) cohorts. Metrics included PCK@0.1/0.2/0.5 and mAP for keypoint detection, mean absolute error (MAE) and correlation coefficient for angle measurement, and diagnostic accuracy, sensitivity, and specificity for clinical outcomes. Baselines comprised ResNet-50, HRNet, and traditional GAT, all evaluated under matched conditions.

